So my fundamental issue is repetition in our data. Since the conversations 
always very structured and quite repetetive, we are suffering literally encoding the 
patters within the model's weights. 
This is a very bad strategy if you want to preform well when something new arises. In recurrent
neural networks we have cell and memory states. These states are updated after each recurrent
step and when used correctly encode the relations between past data. This effectively works as
a long term context. We want to maximally rely on the hidden states and train the model parameters
to rely on the memory.

If the model has a decent understanding of the entire conversation it should be able to complete
single shot packet prediction from the beginning without relying on auto regressive generation.
The past packets context along with the current packet metadata will dictate how this packet should
look right from the beginning.

Here some ideas to accompish this
  - More epochs with variable packets per conversation limit
  - Data augmentation or abberation
  - Reversal, predict the metadata from the payload
  - Explicit past packet encoder
  - External memory unit with reduced model parameters (the only option is to use memory)
  - Reduction of byte embedding dim and increase in hidden size
  - Loss function the penalizes memorization in the weights 
    - Run the model for a number of steps
    - Reset the hidden states
    - Run the prediction again to get our logits
    - Punish a small loss since this implies memorization in the absence of context


